    "title": "Cook review: 'Good Boy' is a fetching time for horror fans, dogs lovers - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Linda Cook ,\n2 hours ago\nDog lovers and horror fans, have I got a movie for you.\nTwo movies, actually. But I’m getting ahead of myself.\n“Good Boy” is a truly unique movie – rather, movies – cleverly directed by Ben Leonberg. His star, Indy – who sorta/kinda plays himself – is the “Good Boy” of the title. He’s also Leonberg’s dog in real life.\n‘Good Boy’ (IMDb) Indy is a Nova Scotia Duck Tolling Retriever with expressions that could move the hardest of hearts. Leonberg wisely keeps the camera mostly at Indy’s height so we continually see the pooch’s point of view.\nLeonberg and Alex Cannon wrote the script, which has minimal dialogue, with a focus on the main human character of Todd (Shane Jensen) and his dog, also named Indy in the film.\nAt the beginning of the movie, Todd is fund, unconscious and bleeding, by his worried sister. He ends up in a hospital, and, once released, decides to go his late grandfather’s isolated home in the woods to recover,\nWe see Todd, but usually his face is blurred or in shadow – so much the better to encourage the audience to focus on Indy and what the dog sees. Indy knows that possibly evil forces are at work in the area and even within the house.\nWe watch Indy as he reacts to sounds, smells and other indicators that Todd doesn’t necessarily notice. As Todd begins to change, Indy becomes truly haunted by his surroundings, which creep into his dreams.\nIs Indy imagining things? His every whimper tells us that something is afoot. Is he simply trying to protect Todd, who spends his time watching VHS tapes of his late grandfather?\nI’m not going to tell you how this ends. But I must tell you there is a little more than you might expect. I stayed through the credits, which run down the left side of the screen as some more video is shown at the right.\nThen the screen goes blank. And suddenly, I was watching another very short, extremely enjoyable movie.\nYou will too, and you’ll have a fetching time.\nRunning time: One hour and 12 minutes.\nRated: PG-13 for foul language, blood, and scary images.\nIn theaters.\nWatch the trailer here .\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WHBF - OurQuadCities.com.",
